{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויקרבו ימי", :translit "va-yikrevu yemei", :gloss "and drew near the days of"} {:he "קמ״ז מזמורים", :translit "kamah-zi mezonot", :gloss "147 psalms"} {:he "שנות יעקב", :translit "shenot ya'akov", :gloss "years of Jacob"} {:he "והמלך דוד זקן בא בימים וגו׳", :translit "ve-ha-melekh david zaken ba ba-yamim ve-gomer", :gloss "and King David was old, advanced in days, etc."}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויקרבו ימי"]}], :frame {:text "Jacob lived 147 years, as he himself states in Genesis 47:28. The Midrash links this number to David's psalms: David composed 147 psalms (excluding those by Asaph, Heman, and Jeduthun), corresponding to Jacob's lifespan, and as a reward David merited that Solomon reigned in his lifetime, just as Jacob reigned alongside Joseph. Jacob died at 147, short of the lifespans of his fathers Abraham (175, Genesis 25:7) and Isaac (180, Genesis 35:28); David too is described as old and advanced in days in 1 Chronicles 23:1.", :sources ["Genesis 47:28" "Genesis 25:7" "Genesis 35:28" "1 Chronicles 23:1"]}} {:quotes [{:he "נא ידך", :translit "na yadekha", :gloss "please your hand"} {:he "מילה", :translit "milah", :gloss "circumcision"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "נא ידך", :b "מילה"}], :frame nil} {:quotes [{:he "חסד ואמת", :translit "chesed ve-emet", :gloss "kindness and truth"} {:he "חסד שעושין עם המתים זהו אמת", :translit "chesed she-osin im ha-metim zehu emet", :gloss "kindness done with the dead, that is truth"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Rashi cites Rabbinic teaching that burying the dead is called חסד ואמת because the recipient cannot reciprocate."}], :frame {:text "Rashi on Genesis 47:29, citing Genesis Rabbah 96:5, explains that חסד שעושין עם המתים אמת הוא — kindness done for the dead is true kindness, because the dead cannot repay it. The phrase חסד ואמת in Genesis 47:29 is thus understood as a single concept: true kindness is burial of the dead